ed via navigator.storage.estimate(), which returns precise byte usage across IndexedDB and CacheStorage.Track file sizes must reflect actual binary payload bytes stored in CacheStorage rather than metadata estimates.Storage eviction uses a Least-Recently-Used (LRU) algorithm. When available space drops below 10%, cached track Blobs are purged from CacheStorage while preserving track metadata records in IndexedDB.4. Riplay SQLite Schema Compatibility and Migration Layer4.1 Schema Extraction and Data MappingRiplay is an open-source music player application built on an Android Room / SQLite database architecture. To enable seamless backup import and export between Muzika and Riplay .db files, Muzika integrates an in-browser SQLite migration pipeline utilizing WebAssembly SQLite (sql.js).The migration process follows a structured pipeline:File Ingestion: The user selects a Riplay .db file via an HTML <input type="file"> control.WASM Initalizing: The file's binary ArrayBuffer is loaded into a sql.js WebAssembly database instance in memory.Table Querying: The migration engine executes SQL queries against Riplay's primary tables: song, artist, album, playlist, and playlist_song_map.Relational Transformation: Row records are extracted, validated, and transformed into Muzika object entities.IndexedDB Commit: Transformed entities are written to Muzika's IndexedDB stores within a single atomic transaction.4.2 Relational Mapping SchemaThe SQL definitions below illustrate the structure of Riplay's core relational schema:SQL-- Riplay Primary Song Entity Table
